  <!-- What You Get -->
  <div class="section" id="what-you-get">
    <h2>What You Get</h2>
    <p>Every board you own builds its own record. A board you have had for two years and run twenty projects on will tell you that. A board you just pulled from a bag will show zero boots and no project history.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- Notes -->
  <div class="section" id="notes">
    <h2>Notes</h2>
    <h3>The one thing that resets it</h3>
    <p>NVS persists across reflashing but not across a full flash erase. If you use Tools in the Arduino IDE to erase all flash, or use esptool to wipe the chip, the board health partition goes with it. That is the only way to reset it to zero, which means it only happens when you choose it to.</p>
    <div class="screenshot-wrap">
      <figure>
        <img src="/images/esp32-memory/board-health-erase-flash.png" alt="Arduino IDE Tools menu showing Erase All Flash Before Sketch Upload set to Disabled" />
        <figcaption>Tools - Erase All Flash Before Sketch Upload. Leave this Disabled to preserve NVS data across reflashing.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
    <h3>Write cycles</h3>
    <p>NVS uses wear levelling to spread writes across the flash cells. Saving uptime every 60 minutes is not going to wear out your flash in any realistic timeframe. If you are concerned, increase the save interval.</p>
  </div>

